{"qid": "musique:6cbb382cf679c0cf091fbf0d3c6d9638bffbc8ea039ca8b2", "question": "Why was Common Sense, by the same author as Rights of Man, an important work?", "answers": ["crystallized the rebellious demand for independence from Great Britain", "Great Britain"], "status": "ok", "correct": true, "answer": "Common Sense, written by Thomas Paine and published on January 10, 1776, was an important work for several reasons[6][7][8][9]:\n\n1. **Catalyzed Independence**: It catalyzed the call for independence from Great Britain, crystallizing sentiment for independence in 1776[6][9].\n\n2. **Widely Read**: It was the most widely read pamphlet of the American Revolution, with virtually every American Patriot reading it. It spread rapidly, selling 100,000 copies in three months to the two million residents of the 13 colonies[6][9].\n\n3. **Spread Republican Ideas**: It was passed around and often read aloud in taverns, contributing significantly to spreading the idea of republicanism and bolstering enthusiasm for separation from Britain[7].\n\n4. **Recruitment Tool**: It encouraged recruitment for the Continental Army[7].\n\n5. **New Argument for Independence**: Paine provided a new and convincing argument for independence by advocating a complete break with history, oriented to the future in a way that compelled the reader[7].\n\n6. **Call for Unity**: It was a clarion call for unity against the corrupt British court, so as to realize America's providential role in providing an asylum for liberty[8].\n\n7. **Paine's Legacy**: Paine's claim to the title \"The Father of the American Revolution\" rests largely on Common Sense, which helped inspire the Patriots to declare independence from Great Britain[6][9].\n\nIn summary, Common Sense was important because it effectively mobilized public opinion in favor of American independence and played a crucial role in the political events leading to the American Revolution.", "tool_calls": ["knowledge_base_search", "knowledge_base_search"], "latency_ms": 42783.7, "error": ""}
{"qid": "musique:73b7efc9538e65dda454b5be9d9b84a35decf082cd4091be", "question": "What is the source of the river Orlam clans crossed to migrate to Namibia?", "answers": ["Thaba Putsoa"], "status": "ok", "correct": false, "answer": "The Orlam clans crossed the **Orange River** to migrate to Namibia[3]. According to historical records, from the late 18th century onward, the Oorlam (Orlam) people from the Cape Colony crossed the Orange River and moved into the area that today is southern Namibia[3].", "tool_calls": ["knowledge_base_search"], "latency_ms": 45993.2, "error": ""}
{"qid": "musique:0da7802e47b081fc2cc573f1d9559abcdd232d4733081512", "question": "Where is the province of Gonbad-e Qabus County located?", "answers": ["in the north-east of the country south of the Caspian Sea", "Caspian Sea"], "status": "ok", "correct": true, "answer": "Gonbad‑e Qabus County (also spelled Gonbad‑e Kavus County) is located in **Golestan Province** in northeastern Iran, southeast of the Caspian Sea[4][7].", "tool_calls": ["kb_wiki_lookup", "knowledge_base_search"], "latency_ms": 20271.3, "error": ""}
